<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hopewell Culture National Historical Park</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mound City Group National Monument, Mound City Group</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hopewell Culture National Historical Park is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> national historical park in Ross County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, that preserves earthworks and burial mounds of the Hopewell culture, which flourished from about 200 BCE to 500 CE. It began as the Mound City Group, much of it destroyed by an army training camp built on the site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, which was declared a National Monument in 1923; in 1992 it was renamed and expanded to include other earthwork and mound groups, among them the Hopewell Mound Group, the culture's type site, and the geometric Seip and Hopeton Earthworks. It is run by the National Park Service and became part of the Hopewell Ceremonial Earthworks World Heritage Site in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hopewell-culture-national-historical-park.jpg" alt="Hopewell Culture National Historical Park" width="330" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ɱ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hopewell_Culture_National_Historical_Park.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
